---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import { countOf } from "../lib/count";

const ideas = await getCollection("concepts");
const philosophers = await getCollection("philosophers");
const stories = await getCollection("stories");
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Little Thinker"
  description="A free library of big questions, written for children. What is on a page, where the ideas come from, and what this site deliberately does not do."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>A library of questions, not answers</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Little Thinker is a free collection of the big questions children ask anyway, written so that a child can
      read a page alone or argue about it with an adult. It is for anyone curious, and it is written for
      children aged about five to twelve.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="detail-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why philosophy, and why for children</h2>
      <p>
        Children ask about fairness, time, death, dreams and whether they are the same person they were last
        year, and they ask without being prompted. Most of the time the answer they get is that it is a silly
        question, or that they will understand when they are older. Neither of those is true. These are the
        questions philosophers have been failing to settle for twenty-five centuries, and a child of seven can
        hold one in their head without any difficulty at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site takes children seriously as thinkers. That means the arguments are real ones and the
        disagreements are left open. It also means we do not pretend that every question has a tidy answer,
        because that would be the one thing guaranteed to make a child stop bothering to ask.
      </p>

      <h2>What is on a page</h2>
      <p>
        Every idea follows the same shape. A question, in one line. A short piece of writing that takes it
        somewhere. A thing to <b>try</b>, which usually needs nothing but a sheet of paper. Three questions to
        <b>talk about</b>, with nothing printed underneath them. The arguments people actually make, under
        <b>Yes, but&hellip;</b>, each with a reply. <b>What other philosophers think</b>, named, with their
        position in a sentence. And <b>if you want to go further</b>, the real book it comes from, so an adult
        who wants to go deeper knows where to go.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the ideas come from</h2>
      <p>
        Every idea and every story names its sources. Nothing here is invented to make a point — if a page
        tells you Plato said something, you can find out where. Where a philosopher's position is given as a
        summary rather than a quotation, the page says so, because a summary and a sentence somebody actually
        wrote are not the same thing and should not be dressed up as each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        The thinkers come from Athens and Jerusalem and Córdoba, from Kalady, Kyoto, Nanjing and Tunis, and
        from people who were arguing with their own traditions rather than only with Europe's.
      </p>

      <h2>What this site does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>No scores, no quizzes.</b> You cannot get these questions wrong.</li>
        <li><b>No explaining the plumbing.</b> This is a place to think about ideas, not about how websites are built.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Use it with a child</h2>
      <p>
        Read a page together and then stop talking. Most of the value is in what happens after the page ends.
        The <a href="/grown-ups">grown-ups page</a> has a ten-minute routine for doing this with one child or
        with a class, and a table of every idea by age and theme for anyone planning lessons.
      </p>

      <h2>Pictures and accuracy</h2>
      <p>
        Portraits and story pictures are public-domain or openly licensed artworks from Wikimedia Commons, and
        each one is credited beside the picture. Where no portrait of a thinker exists that is both
        authentic and free to use, the page uses a drawn mark rather than inventing a face. Stories and accounts
        of people's lives are retold for young readers and are simplified on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are {countOf(ideas.length, "idea")}, {countOf(philosophers.length, "philosopher")} and{" "}
        {countOf(stories.length, "story", "stories")} here so far, and it grows. If something in it is wrong, it is
        worth fixing — which is why the sources are named rather than paraphrased from nowhere.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside class="sidebox">
      <div class="side-block">
        <p class="side-h">Start somewhere</p>
        <a class="side-link" href="/ideas">Big ideas<span>The questions</span></a>
        <a class="side-link" href="/philosophers">Philosophers<span>Who asked them</span></a>
        <a class="side-link" href="/stories">Stories<span>Old tales, open questions</span></a>
      </div>
      <div class="side-block">
        <p class="side-h">For adults</p>
        <a class="side-link" href="/grown-ups">Ten-minute ritual<span>How to run it</span></a>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
